<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Sancocho! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Sancocho!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of phrases with names and dates in a short, simple recording delivered at a slow pace. Interactive: Present responses based on listened questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate understanding of familiar words and phrases with dates and numbers from recordings (e.g., "On Sunday, lunch is at 12."). Can recognize familiar phrases with food names or numbers from simple recordings (e.g., "Three apples, two bananas"). Can create and present responses to questions about food preferences (e.g., "What's his favorite food?" "He likes apples.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher uses flashcards or realia (e.g., an apple, a banana) and pictures of food items like sancocho, rice with chicken, sandwich, soup, water, juice, snack. The teacher shows each item and asks "What's this?" Students listen and repeat the word in chorus. The teacher then introduces "I like..." and "I don't like..." with clear gestures (thumbs up/down) for each food item, e.g., "I like apples! Do you like apples?" (Grammar: WH- and yes/no questions, Present simple; Vocabulary: apple, banana, sandwich, sancocho, rice with chicken, soup, water, juice).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of new vocabulary words: 'apple', 'banana', 'sandwich', 'soup', 'sancocho', 'juice', 'water'. The teacher pays special attention to modeling the /f/ sound in 'favorite' (e.g., "My favorite food is soup.") and the /s/ sound in 'snack'. The teacher also models phonemic awareness by blending spoken phonemes for 'apple' (/ˈæ-p.əl/) and 'water' (/ˈw-ɔːt-ər/) by saying the sounds slowly and then together, having students listen and repeat. (Pronunciation: /f/, /s/, blending).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the food items and the concepts of 'like'/'don't like'.</p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of sancocho: "Is 'sancocho' a drink or a soup?" (Expected answer: "A soup!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of juice and then an apple: "If I say 'I like juice, but I don't like apples', do I eat the apple?" (Expected answer: "No!"). (Grammar: Compound sentences with 'but'; Vocabulary: soup, juice, apple; Pragmatic: Expressing likes and dislikes).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio recording of two children (Ana and Luis) talking about their favorite foods for lunch and snacks. The recording is delivered at a slow pace. (e.g., Ana: "Hi, Luis! What's your favorite food for lunch?" Luis: "My favorite food is sancocho! And I like juice too. But I don't like bananas." Ana: "Oh, really? I like bananas. My favorite snack is an apple. What about you?" Luis: "I like sandwiches for snack. Not apples!").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of phrases with names and dates in a short, simple recording delivered at a slow pace.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the recording for the first time. The teacher asks a gist question: "Are Ana and Luis talking about toys or food?" (Expected answer: "Food!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the recording a second time. The teacher provides a worksheet with pictures of the food mentioned (sancocho, juice, banana, apple, sandwich). Students listen and circle the food items that Ana and Luis talk about.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a third time. The teacher asks: "Who likes sancocho? Ana or Luis?" (Expected answer: "Luis!"). "Who likes apples for snack?" (Expected answer: "Ana!"). (Grammar: WH- questions; Vocabulary: sancocho, juice, banana, apple, sandwich, lunch, snack; Pragmatic: Asking for and giving information about preferences).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Guided practice of new language.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of food preferences using 'I like...', 'I don't like...', 'What's your favorite food?'. Focus on target vocabulary (apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, breakfast, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup) and grammar (Present simple, 'but').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Listening for Specifics):</b> The teacher plays the audio recording again, pausing after each preference is stated. Students have a worksheet with sentences that have blanks. E.g., "Luis's favorite food is ______. He likes ______ too." "Ana likes ______, but she doesn't like ______." Students listen and write the missing food words in the blanks. (Vocabulary: sancocho, juice, banana, apple, sandwich; Grammar: Present simple, Compound sentences with 'but').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Listening and Responding):</b> The teacher prepares two large cards: a green 'LIKE' card and a red 'DON'T LIKE' card. The teacher says sentences about Ana and Luis's food preferences from the audio, some true and some false, at a slow pace. E.g., "Ana likes bananas." "Luis likes apples." Students listen and raise the corresponding 'LIKE' or 'DON'T LIKE' card for what Ana or Luis expressed in the recording. (e.g., if the teacher says "Luis likes apples," students should hold up 'DON'T LIKE' as Luis said "Not apples!"). This activity trains receptive listening to specific phrases and words. (Vocabulary: all core food items; Grammar: Present simple).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Practice - Listening and Confirming):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards for the food items. Student A says a sentence based on the audio (e.g., "Luis likes sancocho."). Student B listens and picks up the correct picture card. Then Student B says a sentence (e.g., "Ana likes apples, but she doesn't like sandwiches.") and Student A listens and points to the correct pictures. This activity focuses on listening for confirmation of preferences. (Vocabulary: all core food items; Grammar: Present simple, Compound sentences with 'but'; Sociolinguistic: Sharing preferences).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and React!" - A guided interactive listening activity where students respond to simple listened questions about food preferences in a small group setting.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher divides the class into small groups (3-4 students). The teacher calls out a student's name from each group and says a short sentence about someone's food preference, then asks a question. For example: "Listen carefully, Group 1! Maria says, 'My favorite food is soup, but I don't like bananas.' (Pause) Juan, does Maria like soup?" Juan (or the student chosen) listens and responds with "Yes, she likes soup!" or "No, she doesn't like soup." The teacher rotates through the groups and names, asking about different food items and preferences (e.g., "He likes rice with chicken and water, but he doesn't like juice. What does he drink?"). The goal is for students to listen for key information and provide a simple, intelligible response. (Skills Focus: Listening for receptive comprehension and presenting responses. Grammar: WH- and yes/no questions, Present simple, Compound sentences with 'but'; Vocabulary: soup, banana, rice with chicken, water, juice, eat, drink, favorite).</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategies and student self-reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Listen and React!" game and the following assessment task. The teacher notes students' ability to listen and correctly identify preferences and provide intelligible responses.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with two empty plates. The teacher plays a new, very short audio clip (e.g., "My name is Carlos. I like apples for snack. I don't like sandwiches. My favorite lunch is rice with chicken."). Students listen and draw an apple on the first plate (liked food) and an 'X' over a sandwich picture on the second plate (disliked food), and draw rice with chicken on the plate for lunch. This assesses receptive listening comprehension of familiar words and phrases.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of liked food from listening input (e.g., drawing an apple).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of disliked food from listening input (e.g., marking 'X' on a sandwich).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of basic food vocabulary (apple, sandwich, rice with chicken).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Circle one: I can hear 'apple'. Yes / No"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Circle one: I can understand when someone says 'I like sancocho'. Yes / No"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the lesson, what worked, and what's next.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food words did I learn to listen for today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or hard to listen for someone's favorite food?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to identify familiar food words from the audio. The pacing of the audio was appropriate for A1.1. Some students needed more repetition for the /f/ and /s/ sounds. The 'Listen and React!' activity successfully prompted simple spoken responses based on listening. For the next lesson, I need to continue reinforcing listening skills while transitioning to reading, ensuring the same vocabulary is visible and recognizable in print. I will prepare visual aids and sentence frames to support reading comprehension of these introduced vocabulary words and simple sentences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a short text or dialogue that includes the same core vocabulary words and grammatical structures from this listening lesson. They will practice reading comprehension of these familiar words to further solidify their understanding of the theme "I Like Sancocho!" and scenario "It's Time to Eat.".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
</table>